

A sandbox has no GitHub API access and no credential for one, so read CI with `grain ci wait`, `grain pr status` and `grain pr base-status` rather than reaching for `gh`, which the stock sandbox image does not carry. Anything else you find missing is usually a Debian package away -- the image keeps no apt package lists, so it is `sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y <package>`, about ten seconds -- and worth naming in your closing note if it is something every run here would want, since a deployment can bake it into its own sandbox image instead. Check with `command -v` before you chain, though: a command that is not installed exits 127, and one at the head of an `&&` chain takes the rest of the chain with it, including the `git commit` at the end of it.

### Grain CLI (`grain`)

Inside your sandbox, the `grain` CLI (run it with `run_command`) reaches this deployment's namespace, CI, pull request, merge queue and the capabilities granted to this task, with no size cap of its own. What `run_command` hands back is still cut to about 20 KB, and counts at that size towards any tool budget the run is under, so send long output to a file and `grep`, `jq` or `head` it rather than printing it all:

- **Namespace** (`ci://`, `src://`, `task://`, `config://`, `memory://`, `deploy://`): `grain ls <uri>`, `grain read <uri> [--tail N]`, `grain grep <re> <uri>`. For example `grain ls ci://acme/widgets`.
- **CI & pull request** (this task's branch; GitHub Actions gates this repository's pull requests): `grain ci wait` (give `run_command` `timeout: 600000`; time blocked is credited back to your deadline), `grain ci list`, `grain ci log [<run-id>] [--job <j>] [--tail N]`, `grain ci test-history <test>`, `grain pr status`, `grain pr base-status`, `grain pr open`, `grain pr reply`, `grain pr resolve`, `grain pr review-comment`. Another task's CI in a repository you may read: `grain ci list --task <id>`, `grain pr status --task <id>` (or `--ref <branch>`), `grain ci log <run-id>`. `grain ci retrigger` re-runs the failed jobs of the GitHub Actions runs at your pull request's head (`--all` every job; `grain ci run-job <job>` one job) without a commit, so the head, its approvals and its reviews stay put -- use it for a flake or a runner fault, never an empty commit. Each run at one commit is re-run at most 3 times, grain's own automatic re-runs of a runner fault included.
- **Task & collaboration:** `grain status "<note>"`, `grain handover write --file <path>`, `grain task wait <id>`, `grain task message <id> "<body>"`, `grain notify --subject <s> --title <t>`, `grain git revert --task <id> --reason "<why>"`, `grain merge-queue`.
- **api grants:** `grain api routes` lists the agent API routes they reach from the sandbox, and `grain api <METHOD> <path> [--body '<json>']` calls one; the `grain-controller` MCP server (`grain_explore_scope`, `grain_invoke`, `grain_list_tasks`, `grain_get_task`, `grain_filing_context`, `grain_submit_task`) calls any authorized route, including model-only scopes.
- **Turn-ending, parking and proposals stay tool calls:** use `comment_on_issue`, `nothing_needed`, `ask_question`, `request_secret`, `request_access`, `wait_for_task` (`park: true`), `resume_later` and `propose_task` directly, not as shell commands.

Run `grain help` or `grain <command> --help` for every flag.